==English==

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|lam|t=flight, escape}}, from {{m|en|lam|t=to thrash, beat}}, from {{inh|en|enm|lamen|t=to injure, maim}}, from {{inh|en|ang|lemian|t=to cripple, make lame, strike}} and {{der|en|non|lemja|t=to beat, disable, suppress}}, related to {{cog|non|lamning|t=a beating}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-on the lam.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Running [[away]], usually from the [[police]]; [[on the run]].
#* '''2012''', The Economist, September 15th, ''[http://www.economist.com/node/21562968 Vietnam: A Tiger at Bay]''
#*: There have been more bank runs, executives '''on the lam''', arrests and credit panics than the country has seen in years.

